        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "7b974c0c-c0c1-5fe7-a54b-c6f92d04648d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Chamelaucium, Vanilla, Verticordia habrantha\n- Plant taxonomy: Myrtaceae, Leptospermum\n- Organizations and projects: HIP Haiti, Vanilla Project\n- Places: south western Western Australia, Coco Beach, Haiti\n\nFacts:\n- Chamelaucium, also known as waxflower, is a genus of shrubs endemic to south western Western Australia.\n- Chamelaucium belongs to the myrtle family Myrtaceae and has flowers similar to those of Leptospermum tea-trees.\n- Vanilla is a flavoring derived from orchids of the genus Vanilla, primarily from the Mexican species V. planifolia.\n- Verticordia habrantha has hairy sepals mostly hidden by round, unfringed petals, so the plant resembles shrubs in the genus Chamelaucium, to which it is closely related.\n- HIP Haiti supports a school and a medical clinic in Coco Beach, Haiti.\n- HIP Haiti started the Vanilla Project to support the local economy sustainably by growing vanilla beans.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "55ee9f81-c78f-5f85-b644-81b6554554b1",
        "text": "Page: Chamelaucium. Sentence: Chamelaucium, also known as waxflower, is a genus of shrubs endemic to south western Western Australia.\nPage: Chamelaucium. Sentence: They belong to the myrtle family Myrtaceae and have flowers similar to those of the tea-trees (\"Leptospermum\").\nPage: Vanilla. Sentence: Vanilla is a flavoring derived from orchids of the genus \"Vanilla\", primarily from the Mexican species, flat-leaved vanilla (\"V. planifolia\").\nPage: Verticordia habrantha. Sentence: Its hairy sepals are mostly hidden by the round, unfringed petals, and as a result, the plant looks like shrubs in the genus \"Chamelaucium\", to which it is closely related.\nPage: HIP Haiti. Sentence: The organization supports a school and a medical clinic in Coco Beach, Haiti, and has started the Vanilla Project, which aims to support the local economy in a sustainable manner by growing vanilla beans.",
